// wdoc section: Cards.
page wdoc_cards { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Cards"

  p "`card` is a diagram box whose body is arbitrary wdoc content — paragraphs, callouts, lists, even nested diagrams. Because it extends `SvgBlock`, a card is a legal child of any `diagram`, `container`, or `timeline`."

  h2 "Free-positioned cards"

  p "Inside a regular `diagram`, a card takes `x`/`y`/`width`/`height` (or anchor fields) like any other shape. Its `body` is wdoc blocks; the renderer wraps the body in an SVG `<foreignObject>` so the rich content scales with the diagram."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
diagram {
  width = 400  height = 200
  card {
    x = 20.0  y = 20.0  width = 160.0  height = 120.0
    body {
      h3 "Service A"
      p "Handles auth, sessions, and rate limiting."
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Timeline cards"

  p "Inside a `timeline`, a card pinned to a date with `on` becomes an event card: the renderer draws a marker + connector and places the card body on the chosen side of the axis. Long-form annotations sit naturally on the axis without crowding the labels."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
timeline {
  start = "2026-01-01"  end = "2026-12-31"
  card {
    on    = "2026-06-15"
    width = 220.0  height = 90.0
    body {
      h3 "Beta release"
      p "Open invites; feedback drives v1 polish."
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  callout "foreignObject" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "Cards are the codebase's only use of <foreignObject>. The body is HTML produced by the same renderer that draws prose pages, so any wdoc block kind can live inside a card."
  }

  h2 "Styling"

  p "The bundled `wdoc-card` stylesheet is structural and neutral. A themed site recolours `.wdoc-card` via the theme's APPLY rule (alongside `.wdoc-map-card`); user `class \"wdoc-card\" { … }` overrides individual properties."

  p "Next: [Tilemaps](wdoc_tilemaps)."
}
